{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>A time to tear.</b> [Regarding] לחשות [to be silent]: [it is] from the root חשה, a past-tense verb in the pattern of העלו. [One should] cast dust on their heads. If it were in the language of command for the future, the he would have a kamatz gadol. The matter is as He said to them, 'Also I knew' — החשו from it [i.e., from that same root]. And behold, Solomon illuminated that even speech has its time."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase עפר על ראשם ('cast dust on their heads') is a well-known idiom for mourning; it is unclear whether Ibn Ezra intends it as a gloss on לקרוע or as a separate remark, so it is rendered hyper-literally. The antecedent of ממנו ('from it') is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:15.992547182Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}